BUILD_ONLY: ghcr.io/mll-lab-nu/eai-robodojo:<images/robodojo/VERSION> is never pushed to a public registry (docs/protocol/README.md
§2.4, D5). Every evaluation host builds it locally; the RoboDojo tasks reference that tag and Harbor finds it in the local
Docker image store.

Why
  The image contains NVIDIA Isaac Sim 5.1 / Omniverse Kit and the pip wheels of the Isaac stack (from pypi.nvidia.com).
  NVIDIA licenses them for use, not for redistribution, so the built image may not be published. Everything else in it may
  be: RoboDojo (github.com/RoboDojo-Benchmark/RoboDojo @ cb2a37d, MIT), its Isaac Lab fork (github.com/yuechen0614/IsaacLab
  @ afca7b0, BSD-3-Clause), its cuRobo fork (github.com/yuechen0614/curobo @ d17b54c, NVIDIA's cuRobo licence), XPolicyLab
  (@ 53c5da5, Apache-2.0; only its client code is imported, and RoboDojo's evaluator is used without a policy server), and
  RoboDojo's assets (Hugging Face dataset RoboDojo-Benchmark/RoboDojo @ 35efbc7, Assets/, Apache-2.0).

What the builder accepts
  - the NVIDIA Isaac Sim / Omniverse license agreement (the image sets OMNI_KIT_ACCEPT_EULA=YES and ACCEPT_EULA=Y, and every
    container that runs it accepts it again at launch);
  - NVIDIA's terms for the isaacsim wheels on pypi.nvidia.com and for cuRobo;
  - the licences above (MIT, BSD-3-Clause, Apache-2.0).
  No account, key or token is needed: everything is downloaded anonymously.

Data
  RoboDojo's assets (Robots, Object, Material, Room, Background, Sensor, Traj, Eval_Layout: 15365 files, ~41 GB) are baked in
  under /opt/RoboDojo/Assets, and the robots' cuRobo configs are generated from their templates as RoboDojo's install does.
  Three MDL materials that two asset USDs load from NVIDIA's asset bucket (images/robodojo/web_assets.txt, each checked
  against its sha256) are baked in under /opt/isaac-assets and the USDs point at the copies (localize_assets.py), and the
  Isaac Lab extension version RoboDojo's fork pins (isaacsim.asset.importer.urdf 2.4.31) is fetched at build time: a task
  container has no general network access (the agent reaches only the model APIs, the verifiers none), and Isaac Sim starts
  and renders offline. RoboDojo's training demonstrations (data/) and policy checkpoints (ckpt/) are NOT in the image. The
  frozen task instances (RoboDojo's own evaluation layouts) live in the task directories.

Host
  Linux x86_64, Docker with the NVIDIA Container Toolkit, an RTX-class NVIDIA GPU with a driver Isaac Sim 5.1 supports (Isaac
  renders with RTX even headless, and RoboDojo runs PhysX with GPU dynamics). Checked on an RTX 4090 (24 GB; AMD Ryzen 9
  7950X, driver 580.159). Build: ~120 GB of free disk during the build (the image is ~74 GB), network access to github.com,
  astral.sh, pypi.org, download.pytorch.org, pypi.nvidia.com, huggingface.co, NVIDIA's extension registry
  (ovextensionsprod.blob.core.windows.net, dw290v42wisod.cloudfront.net) and omniverse-content-production.s3*.amazonaws.com,
  about 60 min. Run: ~5-10 GB of GPU memory and up to ~16 GB of memory per Isaac simulator (a task scene with liquids or
  many objects at the top of the range; scripts/task_resources.toml). Isaac does not exit when the GPU runs out of memory (it waits on a crash dialog): on a shared GPU start fewer at
  once; an agent's own sessions, the standard sidecar and the verifier's replays each need one.

Build (from the repository root)
  docker build -f images/robodojo/Dockerfile -t ghcr.io/mll-lab-nu/eai-robodojo:$(cat images/robodojo/VERSION) .
  The assets stage downloads Assets/ at the pinned dataset revision; with a copy already on disk (same revision), pass it
  instead: --build-context robodojo-assets=<directory that contains Assets/>. The build follows RoboDojo's install.sh
  (torch 2.7.0+cu128, Isaac Sim 5.1.0, RoboDojo's Isaac Lab and cuRobo forks), then sets every Python package to
  images/robodojo/requirements.lock (the reference build's `uv pip freeze`) without resolving and fails unless the
  environment is exactly the lock.

Check a build
  Every frozen instance must rebuild to its recorded state hash. With a task directory at hand:
    t=tasks/robodojo-stack-bowls-i00-privileged
    docker run --rm --gpus all --shm-size 8g -v $PWD/$t:/t ghcr.io/mll-lab-nu/eai-robodojo:$(cat images/robodojo/VERSION) bash -c \
      "cd /tmp && cp /t/environment/rcb_robodojo.py . && python -c \"import numpy as np; np.savez('/tmp/t.npz', \
         actions=np.zeros((0, 14), np.float32), task='stack_bowls')\" && \
       python rcb_robodojo.py replay --task stack_bowls --instance /t/environment/instance.json --traj /tmp/t.npz"
  expects "instance_exact": true (the state hash recorded in instance.json; not for the four tasks graded by verdict, whose
  resets are not bit-identical: instance.json says "determinism": "verdict"). A model's successful trajectory, where the task
  README names one, must replay to success.
